{"meta": {"chapter_title": "Design Draft and Theory-to-Prototype Alignment", "title": "Principle-to-Feature Traceability", "description": "AIL 606 textbook section connecting principle-to-feature traceability to storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next."], "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_design_draft_and_theory_to_prototype_alignment", "ail606_supplement_principle_to_feature_traceability", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 2, "top_tier_hardened": false, "course": "ail606-supplement", "chapter": "04", "section": "02", "prereq_section_ids": ["ail606-supplement/04/01"], "practice_ids": ["ail606_supplement_4_2_principle_to_feature_traceability_artifact_signal", "ail606_supplement_4_2_principle_to_feature_traceability_annotation_signal", "ail606_supplement_4_2_principle_to_feature_traceability_ai_boundary", "ail606_supplement_4_2_principle_to_feature_traceability_calibration"]}, "content": "# Principle-to-Feature Traceability\n\n![Principle-to-Feature Traceability textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/04-02-principle-to-feature-traceability.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nDesigners love to cite principles, but a cited principle and an applied principle look completely different in a prototype. Principle-to-feature traceability is the practice of proving that a named learning principle actually produced a specific feature, and that the feature reduces or strengthens a specific processing demand. Without traceability, a storyboard becomes a pile of nice features each justified after the fact by whichever principle sounds plausible. AIL 606 asks you to build a matrix that survives the reverse question: pick any feature, and you can name the one principle that controls it and the demand it addresses.\n\nThis section teaches you to write traceability rows that resist post-hoc justification. You should leave able to spot the tell of a cosmetic change wearing four principles, and able to rewrite a row so it names one controlling principle, the processing demand it targets, and the feature mechanism.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Map one controlling principle to one feature, with the processing demand named explicitly.\n- Detect post-hoc justification (many principles attached to one cosmetic change).\n- Describe features as mechanisms that act on a demand, not as decoration.\n- Defend any feature by tracing it forward from principle to demand to implementation.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nIn a design crit, a peer presents a matrix where one row reads \"Signaling, Coherence, Modality, Segmenting\" all pointing at \"added a colored highlight and cleaned up the slide.\" The crit goes quiet, because everyone recognizes the pattern: the highlight was added first because it looked good, and four principles were stapled on afterward to make it sound rigorous. The fix is not more principles; it is one principle, honestly chosen, with the demand it reduces stated out loud.\n\nThe case shows why traceability runs in both directions. A forward trace (principle to feature) is easy to fake; the reverse trace (here is the feature, name its one controlling principle and the demand) is the one that exposes whether design reasoning actually happened.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nTraceability is the documented relationship between an instructional principle, the cognitive demand it acts on, and the feature that implements it. The core move is the one-to-one discipline: each feature should be controlled by a single principle. Mayer's principles are not interchangeable adjectives; each names a distinct processing problem. Signaling solves \"the learner cannot tell which element matters.\" Coherence solves \"interesting but irrelevant material is stealing capacity.\" Modality solves \"the visual channel is overloaded because text and graphics compete.\" Redundancy solves \"identical words are presented in two channels at once.\" When you attach four of these to one change, at least three are decorative.\n\nA feature must be written as a mechanism, not an appearance. \"Made it cleaner\" is appearance; \"a yellow highlight appears on exactly the term the narration is speaking, synced to the audio, and clears when narration moves on\" is a mechanism, because it states what the feature does to the learner's attention. Only a mechanism can be tested against the demand it claims to reduce.\n\n| Principle | The processing problem it solves | A mechanism that solves it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Signaling | Learner cannot locate the relevant element | Cue (highlight, arrow, bold) on exactly the active element |\n| Coherence | Extraneous material consumes capacity | Remove decorative images and background music |\n| Modality | Visual channel overloaded by competing text and graphic | Move on-screen text to narration |\n| Segmenting | Continuous presentation outpaces processing | Learner-paced chunks with continue control |\n\nThe reason one-to-one matters is auditability. A reviewer who can isolate the controlling principle can run a clean test: does this mechanism reduce that demand? With four principles fused, no clean test exists, so the claim is unfalsifiable and therefore worthless as evidence of judgment.\n\nEach Mayer principle names one distinct processing problem, not an interchangeable adjective. Match each principle to the single problem it solves; that one-to-one pairing is what the subtraction test later protects.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Signaling\",\"definition\":\"The learner cannot tell which on-screen element matters right now\"},{\"term\":\"Coherence\",\"definition\":\"Interesting but irrelevant material is stealing processing capacity\"},{\"term\":\"Modality\",\"definition\":\"The visual channel is overloaded because text and graphics compete\"},{\"term\":\"Redundancy\",\"definition\":\"Identical words are presented in two channels at the same time\"},{\"term\":\"Segmenting\",\"definition\":\"A continuous presentation outpaces how fast the learner can process it\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A traceability row lists Signaling, Coherence, Modality, and Segmenting all pointing at one change: added a highlight and cleaned up the slide. What does this density most reliably signal?\" explanation=\"Four principles stapled to one cosmetic change is the classic tell of post-hoc justification: the highlight was chosen first because it looked good, and the principles were attached afterward to sound rigorous. At least three are decorative.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Post-hoc justification; the feature came first and the principles were attached to sound rigorous.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Unusually rigorous design reasoning, since more principles is better.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"A correct application, because all four principles always co-occur.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"A formatting error in the table only.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nWork through one annotated before/after revision of a single traceability row, the kind you would defend in a design crit.\n\n**Before (one row of the traceability matrix):**\n\n| Principle | Feature in the storyboard |\n| --- | --- |\n| Signaling, Coherence, Modality, Segmenting | Added a colored highlight to the key term and made the slide cleaner. |\n\n**Annotations a reviewer would leave:**\n\n1. Four principles point at one cosmetic change. That density is a tell that the highlight was chosen first and the principles were attached afterward.\n2. \"Made the slide cleaner\" describes appearance, not a change to what the learner processes.\n3. No row says what demand the feature reduces, so the mapping cannot be tested.\n\n**After (revised row):**\n\n| Principle (controlling) | Demand it addresses | Feature mechanism |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Signaling | Novices scan the whole frame and cannot tell which term the narration is on. | A yellow highlight appears on exactly the term the narration is speaking, synced to the audio, and clears when the narration moves on. |\n\n**Why the after version is stronger:** it names one controlling principle, states the processing demand the feature reduces, and describes the feature as a mechanism (a synced highlight tied to the narration) rather than as decoration. The trace now runs principle and demand to feature, so a reviewer can test whether the highlight actually directs attention. The change is a better argument, not a longer table.\n\nYou are the presenter in the design crit. Work the subtraction test live and decide how to defend the row.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Defending the four-principle row in crit\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"A peer points at your row, where Signaling, Coherence, Modality, and Segmenting all map to one change: a colored highlight plus a cleaner slide. How do you respond?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Run the subtraction test out loud: remove each principle and keep the one the feature cannot do without\",\"next\":\"n2\",\"feedback\":\"Right move. Subtraction is faster and more honest than justifying additions.\"},{\"label\":\"Defend all four by explaining how each could plausibly apply\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"This is principle inflation; plausibility is exactly what lets a cosmetic change wear four citations.\"},{\"label\":\"Add a fifth principle to show thoroughness\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"More citations deepen the post-hoc problem rather than fixing it.\"}]},\"n2\":{\"prompt\":\"Removing Coherence, Modality, and Segmenting leaves the highlight intact, but removing Signaling makes it pointless. Now how do you write the feature cell?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"As a mechanism: a yellow highlight appears on exactly the term the narration is speaking, synced to the audio, and clears when narration moves on\",\"next\":\"n4\",\"feedback\":\"This states what the feature does to attention, so the claim becomes testable.\"},{\"label\":\"As an appearance: made the key term stand out and the slide cleaner\",\"next\":\"n5\",\"feedback\":\"Appearance words cannot be traced to a demand, because looking nice is not a cognitive event.\"}]},\"n3\":{\"prompt\":\"The crit stalls; nobody can name a clean test for the row. What does this prove about the row?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"With principles fused, no clean test exists, so the claim is unfalsifiable and worthless as evidence\",\"next\":\"n2\",\"feedback\":\"Exactly. Back up and run the subtraction test instead.\"}],\"feedback\":\"Fusion makes the row unauditable.\"},\"n4\":{\"prompt\":\"Final step: state the learner-side demand the row addresses.\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Novices scan the whole frame and cannot tell which term the narration is on\",\"next\":\"end\",\"feedback\":\"Stated from the learner side, this demand can be checked in user testing.\"},{\"label\":\"The slide had too much clutter\",\"next\":\"end\",\"feedback\":\"That is the designer view; the learner-side demand is what makes the row falsifiable.\"}]},\"n5\":{\"prompt\":\"A reviewer asks you to test whether the feature worked. Can you?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"No; an appearance claim has no demand to test against, so rewrite it as a mechanism\",\"next\":\"n4\",\"feedback\":\"Correct; only a mechanism acting on attention can be tested.\"}],\"feedback\":\"Rewrite as a mechanism, then name the demand.\"},\"end\":{\"prompt\":\"The row now names one controlling principle, a learner-side demand, and a synced-highlight mechanism. It survives the reverse trace.\",\"feedback\":\"Sparse and falsifiable beats crowded and decorative.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBuild the matrix so it survives the reverse trace. Before the storyboard ships, audit every row with three tests:\n\n1. **One-principle check:** Can you cut the row to a single controlling principle? If three drop away with no loss, they were decorative.\n2. **Mechanism check:** Does the feature column say what the feature does to attention or processing, not how it looks?\n3. **Demand check:** Does the row name the specific demand the feature reduces or strengthens, so the claim can be tested?\n\nIf a row fails any test, it is a justification, not a trace.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe frequent error is principle inflation: attaching every plausible principle to a feature so the matrix looks thorough. Thoroughness here is a liability, because it signals that the principles were chosen to flatter the feature rather than to control it. A related error is describing features by appearance (\"cleaner,\" \"more modern\"), which can never be traced to a demand because appearance is not a cognitive event.\n\nA matrix is strong when each row is sparse and falsifiable, not when each row is crowded with citations.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nStart from a finished feature and try to delete principles. Take the row, and one at a time, remove each principle and ask whether the feature still makes sense without it. The principle you cannot remove is the controlling one; the ones that fall away without consequence were decorative. This subtractive method is faster and more honest than trying to justify additions.\n\nNext, rewrite the feature cell as a mechanism. Replace any appearance word with a verb describing what happens to the learner: directs attention to, removes from the channel, paces, separates. If you cannot write the verb, you do not yet understand what the feature does, which is the real finding.\n\nFinally, name the demand in the learner's terms, not the designer's. \"Reduces clutter\" is the designer's view; \"novices cannot tell which term the narration is on\" is the learner's processing problem. The demand cell is where the row becomes testable, because a demand stated from the learner's side can be checked in user testing.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: **claim** on the left, **evidence** on the right. The claim is the single controlling principle you believe governs a feature; the evidence is the mechanism and the demand that make the mapping testable. By the end of AIL 606 04.02, your notes should let a reviewer audit any row by running the reverse trace.\n\nA strong reader of a traceability matrix can answer four questions:\n\n1. For this feature, which one principle survives the subtraction test?\n2. Is the feature written as a mechanism or as an appearance?\n3. What demand, stated from the learner's side, does the row claim to reduce?\n4. How would you test in user data whether the mechanism worked?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact is strong when every row is sparse (one controlling principle), mechanistic (a verb acting on attention), and falsifiable (a named demand a reviewer could test). Treat a crowded, citation-heavy matrix as a warning sign, not a strength. Where a row still fuses principles, mark it with the annotation tools and ask BigAL to compare a one-principle row against a four-principle row rather than for a generic definition of signaling.\n\nA useful final paragraph for the artifact would sound like this: \"I rewrote the signaling row to name one principle, the demand it reduces, and the synced-highlight mechanism. I dropped Coherence, Modality, and Segmenting from that row because removing them changed nothing about why the highlight exists.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same matrix from a narrated slide deck to a text-only print job aid where there is no audio channel. The signaling row may survive (a bold cue still directs attention), but the Modality and Redundancy rows lose their basis, because there is no second channel to balance or duplicate. Transfer means re-deriving which principles even apply in the new channel mix, not copying the rows. If you understand traceability, removing the audio channel visibly prunes the matrix.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/04-02-principle-to-feature-traceability-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"04.02\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes for AIL 606 04.02. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_principle_to_feature_traceability\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_principle_to_feature_traceability\", \"ail606_supplement_design_draft_and_theory_to_prototype_alignment\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm that every row in your matrix names one controlling principle, a learner-side demand, and a feature mechanism, so a reviewer could run the reverse trace on any row and find it falsifiable.\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "ail606_supplement_4_2_principle_to_feature_traceability_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_principle_to_feature_traceability", "stem": "A traceability row reads: Principle = Mayer's signaling principle; Feature = \"we added color.\" Which rewrite makes the row defensible?", "options": ["Principle = signaling; Feature = we used a nicer color palette across the deck.", "Principle = signaling; Feature = a yellow highlight cues the one term the narration is explaining at that moment, so attention is directed instead of left to scan the whole frame.", "Principle = signaling; Feature = the slide looks more modern now.", "Principle = signaling; Feature = AI suggested the color, so we kept it."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Traceability requires the feature to be the operational mechanism of the principle. Signaling directs attention to essential material at the right moment, not decoration.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_4_2_traceability_decoration_as_principle", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_principle_to_feature_traceability"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_4_2_principle_to_feature_traceability_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_principle_to_feature_traceability", "stem": "Which direction should a sound traceability argument run?", "options": ["Feature to principle: build the feature you like, then find a principle that sounds like it justifies it.", "Principle to feature: name the learning principle and the demand it addresses, then show the specific feature that operationalizes it.", "Either direction works as long as both are named somewhere in the matrix.", "Principle to aesthetic: pick the principle that matches the chosen visual style."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Post-hoc principle-fitting rationalizes a feature you already wanted. Traceability is meaningful only when the principle and its demand drive the feature.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_4_2_traceability_post_hoc_justification", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_principle_to_feature_traceability"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_4_2_principle_to_feature_traceability_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ai_supported_learning", "stem": "You ask an AI tool to map your storyboard features to multimedia principles. It returns a clean table citing the coherence and modality principles. What is the strongest bounded use?", "options": ["Accept the table because every row cites a real principle.", "Check each row for a controlling principle per feature and reject any row where the cited principle does not actually describe what the feature does to the learner's processing.", "Add every principle the AI lists so the table looks thorough.", "Ask the AI to also score each mapping out of ten and report the average."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "AI often attaches plausible-sounding principle labels that do not match the feature's actual mechanism. The designer must audit each mapping against the principle's real definition.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_4_2_traceability_ai_label_matching", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ai_supported_learning"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_4_2_principle_to_feature_traceability_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "evidence_based_revision", "stem": "A reviewer notes that your matrix \"maps every feature to three principles each.\" Why might this be a weakness rather than a strength?", "options": ["Three principles per feature is always too many to defend.", "Stacking many principles onto one feature often hides that the feature was not designed from any single principle's demand, making the mapping decorative rather than diagnostic.", "Reviewers prefer one principle per matrix, not per feature.", "It makes the matrix too long to fit on one page."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Dense many-to-one mappings usually signal post-hoc labeling. A diagnostic matrix names the controlling principle and the demand it addresses, even if a feature touches others secondarily.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_4_2_traceability_principle_overstacking", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["evidence_based_revision"]}]}, "content_ver